// "Gradual verification": a `where` refinement predicate is PROVEN
// outright when it cheaply can be, skipping the runtime check entirely --
// zero overhead, the actual payoff of doing this gradually instead of as
// a bare assert -- and falls back to an ordinary runtime check otherwise.
// Same three-way shape as gradual TYPING's own Dyn-boundary Check nodes:
// proven statically (nothing inserted), refuted statically (a real error,
// here at parse time since the value's already known), or deferred to
// runtime (an unannotated/Dyn-sourced value's real type/shape).
//
// renno has no `>`/`<=`/`>=` yet, so a predicate leans on `<`/`==` and
// arithmetic (`0 < n` for "n is positive") -- but `&&`/`||`/`!` DO exist,
// and compound predicates are just as provable: try_eval_bool recognizes
// the `if` shape they desugar into and evaluates through it.

// Proven at parse time: 50 is a known literal, `0 < 50 && 50 < 100` is
// decidable right here, so this `let` compiles to EXACTLY `let n = 50 in
// n + 1` -- no wrapping `if`/`fail` at all.
let proven = let n: Int where 0 < n && n < 100 = 50 in n + 1 in

// Not provable (the parameter's actual value is unknown until a caller
// supplies one) -- becomes a real runtime check: `if 0 < n && n < 100
// then n * 2 else fail(...)`.
let double_in_range = fun n: Int where 0 < n && n < 100 -> n * 2 in

proven + double_in_range(3)
